### Base URL
@baseUrl = http://localhost:4000/api

### Tokens — paste from /admin/auth/login and /customers/auth/login
@adminToken = PASTE_ADMIN_TOKEN
@customerToken = PASTE_CUSTOMER_TOKEN

### Fill from your own catalogue — GET /inventory/getInventory lists them
@variantId = 8f2a1c44-1c0c-4511-b091-246a213e8373
@otherVariantId = 6b13d902-77aa-4f22-9c31-5d2b0e441a90
@orderId = PASTE_ORDER_ID


### ============================================================
### ORDERS  (F-07)
###
### This is what replaces the WhatsApp hand-off on the bag page.
### The bag used to be formatted into a message and opened in
### wa.me; it now becomes a row in `orders` with its lines in
### `order_items`, and the shop works from the admin queue.
###
### Two rules run through every endpoint below:
###
###   Prices come from the database, never the request. A line is
###   a variantId and a quantity. There is no field to send a
###   price in, which is stronger than checking one.
###
###   Placing an order reserves stock, in the same transaction.
###   Two shoppers racing for the last piece resolve rather than
###   both succeeding — the second is refused with the count.
###   Cancelling puts it back.
###
### Status has two axes, because they move independently:
###
###   status          pending_payment → confirmed → packed
###                   → shipped → delivered, or cancelled
###   payment_status  pending / paid / failed / refunded
###
### Cancelling is reachable up to `packed` and from nowhere
### after — once it is with the courier it is a return, not a
### cancellation.
### ============================================================


### ============================================================
### PLACE AN ORDER — as a guest
###
### No Authorization header at all. The bag page promises
### checkout without an account, so this is a supported path,
### not a fallback. The order carries its contact snapshot and
### is found later by number + email.
### ============================================================
POST {{baseUrl}}/orders/placeOrder
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "contact": {
    "name": "Meera Krishnan",
    "email": "meera@example.com",
    "phone": "9876543210"
  },
  "shippingAddress": {
    "line1": "14 Bharathi Street",
    "line2": "Gandhipuram",
    "landmark": "Opposite the water tank",
    "city": "Coimbatore",
    "state": "Tamil Nadu",
    "postalCode": "641012"
  },
  "items": [
    { "variantId": "{{variantId}}", "quantity": 1 }
  ],
  "customerNote": "Please deliver after 6pm"
}


### ============================================================
### PLACE AN ORDER — signed in
###
### Identical body. The only difference is the header: the order
### attaches to the account the token names, which is what makes
### it show up under /getMyOrders. `customerId` is never read
### from the body — a shopper must not be able to file an order
### under somebody else's account.
### ============================================================
POST {{baseUrl}}/orders/placeOrder
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{customerToken}}

{
  "contact": {
    "name": "Meera Krishnan",
    "email": "meera@example.com",
    "phone": "9876543210"
  },
  "shippingAddress": {
    "line1": "14 Bharathi Street",
    "city": "Coimbatore",
    "state": "Tamil Nadu",
    "postalCode": "641012"
  },
  "items": [
    { "variantId": "{{variantId}}", "quantity": 2 },
    { "variantId": "{{otherVariantId}}", "quantity": 1 }
  ]
}


### ============================================================
### PLACE AN ORDER — rejected: not enough stock
###
### 409, naming the piece and the count left, so the bag can say
### "only 2 left of this" rather than "checkout failed".
### ============================================================
POST {{baseUrl}}/orders/placeOrder
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "contact": {
    "name": "Meera Krishnan",
    "email": "meera@example.com",
    "phone": "9876543210"
  },
  "shippingAddress": {
    "line1": "14 Bharathi Street",
    "city": "Coimbatore",
    "state": "Tamil Nadu",
    "postalCode": "641012"
  },
  "items": [
    { "variantId": "{{variantId}}", "quantity": 19 }
  ]
}


### ============================================================
### PLACE AN ORDER — rejected: the same size twice
###
### 400. "2 × M" and "1 × M, 1 × M" are the same order to a
### shopper and two rows to every report, so one line per size is
### enforced in the validator and again by a unique index.
### ============================================================
POST {{baseUrl}}/orders/placeOrder
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "contact": {
    "name": "Meera Krishnan",
    "email": "meera@example.com",
    "phone": "9876543210"
  },
  "shippingAddress": {
    "line1": "14 Bharathi Street",
    "city": "Coimbatore",
    "state": "Tamil Nadu",
    "postalCode": "641012"
  },
  "items": [
    { "variantId": "{{variantId}}", "quantity": 1 },
    { "variantId": "{{variantId}}", "quantity": 1 }
  ]
}


### ============================================================
### TRACK AN ORDER — no account
###
### A POST although it reads: the email is the second factor and
### does not belong in a query string. Both a wrong number and a
### wrong email return the same 404, so this cannot be used to
### test whether an order number exists.
### ============================================================
POST {{baseUrl}}/orders/trackOrder
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "orderNumber": "SB-001001",
  "email": "meera@example.com"
}


### ============================================================
### MY ORDERS — storefront token
### ============================================================
GET {{baseUrl}}/orders/getMyOrders
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{customerToken}}


### ============================================================
### MY ORDERS — only the ones on their way
### ============================================================
GET {{baseUrl}}/orders/getMyOrders?status=shipped&limit=10
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{customerToken}}


### ============================================================
### ONE ORDER
###
### Either token works. A shopper gets their own and a 404 for
### anyone else's — 404 rather than 403, because "that order
### exists but is not yours" confirms it exists.
### ============================================================
GET {{baseUrl}}/orders/getOrderById/{{orderId}}
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{customerToken}}


### ============================================================
### THE ADMIN QUEUE — newest first
### ============================================================
GET {{baseUrl}}/orders/getAllOrders
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}


### ============================================================
### WHAT NEEDS PACKING
###
### Paid and accepted, not yet packed — the queue the shop works
### from in the morning. Oldest first, because within a status
### the oldest order is the most urgent.
### ============================================================
GET {{baseUrl}}/orders/getAllOrders?status=confirmed&sort=oldest
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}


### ============================================================
### FIND AN ORDER FROM A PHONE CALL
###
### Searches the order number, name, email and phone. "1042"
### finds SB-001042.
### ============================================================
GET {{baseUrl}}/orders/getAllOrders?search=9876543210
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}


### ============================================================
### THE TILES
###
### Counts and revenue per status over the whole book, not the
### current page. Revenue excludes cancelled and unpaid orders —
### an order awaiting payment is a hope, not a sale.
### ============================================================
GET {{baseUrl}}/orders/getOrderSummary
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}


### ============================================================
### PAYMENT RECEIVED
###
### pending_payment → confirmed, and payment_status → paid, in
### one statement. This is the only point where the money and the
### parcel are known to agree, which is why they move together.
###
### `reference` is the UPI or bank reference — optional, because
### the shop may be looking at a screenshot, but it is the only
### thread back from an order to the transfer that paid for it.
###
### When a gateway lands its webhook calls the same service
### method and this stays as the manual fallback.
### ============================================================
POST {{baseUrl}}/orders/confirmPayment/{{orderId}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

{
  "reference": "UPI 428193746512"
}


### ============================================================
### PACKED
### ============================================================
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/orders/updateOrderStatus/{{orderId}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

{
  "status": "packed"
}


### ============================================================
### SHIPPED
### ============================================================
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/orders/updateOrderStatus/{{orderId}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

{
  "status": "shipped",
  "note": "DTDC, docket 7719004421"
}


### ============================================================
### DELIVERED
### ============================================================
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/orders/updateOrderStatus/{{orderId}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

{
  "status": "delivered"
}


### ============================================================
### AN ILLEGAL MOVE
###
### 409, saying what this order can actually become. An order
### that jumped from pending_payment to delivered would have
### skipped taking the money, and the admin panel is a screen
### with buttons on it.
### ============================================================
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/orders/updateOrderStatus/{{orderId}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

{
  "status": "delivered"
}


### ============================================================
### CANCEL — by the shopper
###
### Only while it is still awaiting payment. After that the shop
### has the money and has started picking, and cancelling is a
### conversation, not a button. The reason is optional here — a
### shopper who changes their mind owes nobody an explanation.
###
### Whatever cancels it, the stock goes back on the shelf in the
### same transaction.
### ============================================================
POST {{baseUrl}}/orders/cancelOrder/{{orderId}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{customerToken}}

{
  "reason": "Ordered the wrong size"
}


### ============================================================
### CANCEL — by the shop
###
### Reachable up to `packed`. The reason is required from an
### admin: the customer will ask about this next week, and
### "cancelled, no reason recorded" is not an answer.
### ============================================================
POST {{baseUrl}}/orders/cancelOrder/{{orderId}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

{
  "reason": "The last piece was damaged in storage — refunded in full"
}
